<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

<h3>How fast should I act if I notice sudden hearing loss in one ear?</h3>
<p>Treat it as urgent and call an audiologist or ENT right away—ideally within 24–72 hours. Earlier evaluation is linked to better odds of recovery for sudden sensorineural hearing loss.</p>

<h3>Will a CROS device make things sound totally normal again?</h3>
<p>CROS systems don’t restore true stereo hearing. They send sound from your poorer side to your better ear, which can dramatically improve access to speech and reduce the “I can’t hear you on that side” problem. Many people find them life-changing in groups and at work.</p>

<h3>Can a smartphone hearing test tell me if one ear is worse?</h3>
<p>Some apps and headphones can flag a difference, but they aren’t a substitute for a clinical audiogram. If an app suggests asymmetry—or you notice it yourself—schedule a professional hearing test.</p>

<h3>Does tinnitus in one ear mean I have a tumor?</h3>
<p>Most unilateral tinnitus isn’t caused by a tumor, but it can be a reason to get checked, especially if it’s new, pulsing with your heartbeat, or paired with unequal hearing. An audiologist and ENT can determine if imaging is appropriate.</p>


